WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.163
<v ->Okay, so let's get into AWS CodeDeploy.</v>

2
00:00:03.163 --> 00:00:05.190
CodeDeploy is obviously going to be

3
00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:08.910
a better focus on the DevOps Pro exam, but for the SA Pro

4
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.850
you absolutely need to know how CodeDeploy works

5
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:15.780
for deploying to EC2, ASG, ECS and Lambda.

6
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.480
And this is what this lecture is going to cover.

7
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.090
So CodeDeploy is going to help us deploy our applications

8
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.820
to, for example, many EC2 instances.

9
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.700
The idea is that they're all on the V1 version

10
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.830
and we want to somehow migrate them

11
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.370
to a V2 version by deploying new versions of the code.

12
00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:35.452
These instances are not managed by Elastic Beanstalk.

13
00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:39.201
They are running the DevOps, the CodeDeploy agents

14
00:00:39.201 --> 00:00:41.220
and this CodeDeploy agent is going to be

15
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.910
the one helping these instances move on to the next version.

16
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.351
So there's several ways to do deployments on AWS.

17
00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:50.070
There is Ansible, Terraform, Chef, Puppet, et cetera

18
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:51.300
and even Beanstalk.

19
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.570
But we can use the managed service AWS could deploy to

20
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.270
do this specifically on AWS.

21
00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.550
Why? Because CodeDeploy has integrations

22
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:04.020
with EC2 auto scaling in groups ECS and Lambda.

23
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.850
So let's have a deeper look at

24
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.530
to as to how CodeDeploy works

25
00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.650
for each and every one of these functions.

26
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.510
So the first one is EC2.

27
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.782
We define how we want to deploy the application

28
00:01:14.782 --> 00:01:19.680
in the file named appspec.yml + deployment strategy

29
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.640
and deployment strategy

30
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.590
could be how fast we want the rollout to be.

31
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.550
Is it all at once?

32
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.540
Is it gradual?

33
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:25.800
And this is not the focus of this.

34
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.350
We just need to understand how it happens at a high level.

35
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.060
It will do in place updates

36
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.800
to your fleet of EC2 instances.

37
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.590
It doesn't create new EC2 instances.

38
00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.410
And we can define hooks on the EC2 instance to

39
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.510
verify the deployment is working great

40
00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.340
after each deployment phase.

41
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.010
So the idea for one example is called half at a time.

42
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.042
We have four EC2 instances, they're V1

43
00:01:46.042 --> 00:01:48.882
and half of them are going to be taken offline.

44
00:01:48.882 --> 00:01:50.850
So we have two of them gone.

45
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.980
Then two of them are kept on v1.

46
00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.290
So right now we're serving half the traffic.

47
00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.970
Okay? So we may have more load

48
00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.129
onto these two remaining EC2 instances,

49
00:01:59.129 --> 00:02:00.810
but because these two,

50
00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.279
the other ones have been taken offline

51
00:02:02.279 --> 00:02:04.980
then they can be upgraded into a V2 version.

52
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.610
So now we're running half of the EC2 instances

53
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.910
on V2 and half of the instances on V1.

54
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.760
Then we'll take the other half, we'll take it down.

55
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.950
I mean CodeDeploy will do this for you obviously.

56
00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.520
And then the instances will be upgraded to v2.

57
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.950
And we have successfully performed half

58
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.220
at a time CodeDeploy deployments into EC2.

59
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.630
There are different strategies that EC2

60
00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.660
CodeDeploy can do for EC2, but just remember this one.

61
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.330
It is very simple and it gives you an idea of the process.

62
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.210
In this example, this is in place updates.

63
00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.150
So the EC2 instance is going to be changed to take

64
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.935
down the V1 application in some way

65
00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:44.670
and make the V2 application appear right after.

66
00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.470
Okay, let's step it up a little bit

67
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:50.040
and talk about CodeDeploy to your auto scaling groups.

68
00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.040
So you have two kind of updates to your auto scaling group.

69
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.440
The first one is in place updates where

70
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.680
you update the current existing EC2 instances

71
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.090
in your auto scaling group.

72
00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.860
So this is very, very similar

73
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.050
to what we've seen from the past slide.

74
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.790
And the instances that get created

75
00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.820
by an ASG will also get automated deployment.

76
00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.670
So if your ASG does a scale out

77
00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.340
so it creates a new EC2 instance during

78
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.423
an CodeDeploy deployment that could deploy will also deploy

79
00:03:17.423 --> 00:03:19.620
through that new EC2 instance.

80
00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.450
So this is one way, and I'm not representing it

81
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.550
because we've seen it in the past slide

82
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.855
but then there is blue green deployment

83
00:03:25.855 --> 00:03:29.250
and in that case a new auto scaling group is created

84
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.447
the settings are copied

85
00:03:30.447 --> 00:03:32.640
and then the instances will be transferred

86
00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.500
from one auto scaling group to another.

87
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.860
And we can choose how long to keep the old instances

88
00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.380
and we must be using an elastic load balancer

89
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.839
for this kind of deployments.

90
00:03:41.839 --> 00:03:42.672
So I've simplified it, but here is the example.

91
00:03:42.672 --> 00:03:44.679
So ALB is serving traffic

92
00:03:44.679 --> 00:03:49.530
and we currently have an auto scaling group that has EC2

93
00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.830
instances that are launched using the launch template V1

94
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:56.040
and CodeDeploy will deploy your application

95
00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.300
into these instances

96
00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.920
in case your auto scaling group

97
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.900
starts adding more EC2 instance.

98
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.103
So as we scale out for our auto scaling group,

99
00:04:03.103 --> 00:04:04.680
CodeDeploy is smart enough

100
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.890
to start deploying the application to the instances.

101
00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.070
But as soon as we initiate a deployment for getting

102
00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.520
into a v2, we're getting into a blue/greenreen deployment

103
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.010
and EC2 instances are going to be created

104
00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.180
in another auto scaling group.

105
00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.370
But in this example are just put it under the same

106
00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:21.330
it doesn't really matter

107
00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.820
and they get transferred over to the old auto scaling group.

108
00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:24.990
And so now we have

109
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.512
both EC2 instances running the V2 and the V1.

110
00:04:28.512 --> 00:04:31.130
And so CodeDeploy has to deploy the application

111
00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.770
to these new EC2 instances running the V2 version.

112
00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.661
And then our ALB is going to be serving both versions

113
00:04:38.661 --> 00:04:40.140
at the same time.

114
00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.660
And if everything looks fine, then at some point

115
00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:46.324
CodeDeploy is going to remove the old EC2 instances

116
00:04:46.324 --> 00:04:48.870
and the deployment will have happened.

117
00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.240
So what we can remember out of this deployment is

118
00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.030
that if we're doing blue/green deployment with an ASG

119
00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.300
new EC2 instances are created and so they're clean.

120
00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.060
And then we just create the application V2

121
00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.980
on these new EC2 instances.

122
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.080
And that may be easier than shutting

123
00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.360
down the V1 instance of the V1 application

124
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.900
and creating the V2 application on the same instance.

125
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.090
So it's up to you, it's up to your requirements, but

126
00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.660
at least you've seen the blue/green deployment on the ASG.

127
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.980
Next, let's talk about Lambda.

128
00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.020
So CodeDeploy can deploy

129
00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.090
to AWS Lambda and you need to know this.

130
00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.110
And what it will do is

131
00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.780
that it will use the traffic shifting feature

132
00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.820
of your Lambda alias.

133
00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:27.795
You can also define a pre

134
00:05:27.795 --> 00:05:31.140
and post-traffic hook Lambda functions that will

135
00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.570
help you validate the deployment itself.

136
00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.082
I'll show you this in a diagram right now

137
00:05:35.082 --> 00:05:37.170
and you can also get easy

138
00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.930
and automated rollbacks using a CloudWatch alarm

139
00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.100
that you would set up.

140
00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.140
And finally, if you're using the serverless

141
00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.660
application model framework SAML, then

142
00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.570
in case you deploy new Lambda versions through SAML

143
00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:51.960
it will natively use CodeDeploy for doing the deployment.

144
00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.670
And that is very important to remember.

145
00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.027
So let's go through a concrete example.

146
00:05:56.027 --> 00:05:57.570
We have our Lambda alias

147
00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.210
and it's running a V1 Lambda function.

148
00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.340
And so we're going to trigger CodeDeploy, maybe

149
00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:05.247
it's our CICD that's going to to trigger CodeDeploy.

150
00:06:05.247 --> 00:06:09.690
And CodeDeploy needs to create the V2 function

151
00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:10.639
of our Lambda.

152
00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:13.650
And right now it's going to be in the alias, but with 0%.

153
00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.033
So no traffic is going to go to our V2.

154
00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:19.222
CodeDeploy will then run if we have specified it

155
00:06:19.222 --> 00:06:21.621
a Lambda function, which is another Lambda function.

156
00:06:21.621 --> 00:06:23.880
And that Lambda function is called

157
00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.220
a pre- traffic hook function.

158
00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.792
And its role is to run some tests

159
00:06:27.792 --> 00:06:30.180
on your V2 Lambda function to make sure

160
00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.370
that it behaves properly.

161
00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.740
If it does behave properly and it passes the test

162
00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.590
then CodeDeploy will start using the Lambda alias

163
00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.731
traffic shifting feature to start shifting traffic

164
00:06:40.731 --> 00:06:43.740
between your V1 function and your V2 function.

165
00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.360
And so now your V2 function is going to

166
00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.070
start receiving traffic.

167
00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.440
You can also set up a CloudWatch alarm that is

168
00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.030
going to monitor maybe some metrics

169
00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.110
around your Lambda functions

170
00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.050
or other things such as if the CloudWatch alarm

171
00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.360
the CloudWatch alarm takes off, then CodeDeploy

172
00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.580
will automatically roll back your deployment

173
00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.040
and then you'll be back with just the V1 function.

174
00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.890
Finally, once the traffic fix shifting has done

175
00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.730
and is finished, we can run a post traffic hook.

176
00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.320
This is one more Lambda function

177
00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.500
that we have to define optionally that will run some tests.

178
00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.390
After all the traffic has been shifted

179
00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.088
onto our V2 Lambda function.

180
00:07:17.088 --> 00:07:21.270
And if everything passes, then our V1 Lamda function is gone

181
00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.690
and the Lambda alias only serves the V2 Lambda function.

182
00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.490
And that's it, fairly simple.

183
00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:29.040
It looks like the same from before, but here you

184
00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:31.230
have to remember we're using the traffic shifting feature

185
00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.610
of the Lambda alias.

186
00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.610
And also we have Lambda functions for pre-traffic hook

187
00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.250
and post-traffic hook to run some tests during

188
00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.390
our deployments.

189
00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:43.740
Finally, we have CodeDeploy into ECS, and this is

190
00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.950
supported again with blue/green deployments for Amazon ECS

191
00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.140
and AWS Fargate.

192
00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:50.850
And the setup is going to be done

193
00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.430
within the ECS service definition.

194
00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:54.780
So it's not something you can do currently

195
00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.160
from the CodeDeploy console.

196
00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.980
You have to go into the ECS service and define it there.

197
00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:02.460
The idea is that a new task set will be created

198
00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.210
and then the traffic will be rerouted to the new task set.

199
00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.594
And then after the deployment has been done

200
00:08:08.594 --> 00:08:12.060
CodeDeploy will check if everything is stable for X number

201
00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:12.900
of minutes.

202
00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.050
And then if it is, then the old task set is going to

203
00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:18.720
be terminated so you have some time to notice issues

204
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:19.890
if you need to.

205
00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:22.740
And another strategy is to use Canary deployments so

206
00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.920
that a Canary is used for 10% for five minutes

207
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:29.586
and then everything is shifted over to the new deployments.

208
00:08:29.586 --> 00:08:31.620
So let's go through a diagram again.

209
00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.855
So our application answer is going to be linked

210
00:08:34.855 --> 00:08:35.688
to an ECS service.

211
00:08:35.688 --> 00:08:38.730
The ECS service is going to launch ECS task using the task

212
00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:42.270
definition number one, and then CodeDeploy is going to say

213
00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.615
Hey, I want to do a deployment onto ECS.

214
00:08:44.615 --> 00:08:48.870
You need to upgrade to a new task version task definition.

215
00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.850
So there's going to be a blue/green deployment

216
00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.130
and a traffic shifting feature that will happen.

217
00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:56.037
And so ECS service itself will start launching

218
00:08:56.037 --> 00:08:59.287
a new task set using task definition number 2

219
00:08:59.287 --> 00:09:02.580
and that will be linked into the application balancer.

220
00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.350
And then CodeDeploy, we'll make sure

221
00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.810
that there is a traffic shifting feature happening.

222
00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.510
So this is a screenshot from CodeDeploy itself.

223
00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.203
And as you can see on the bottom right

224
00:09:11.203 --> 00:09:13.800
there's a traffic shifting progress, which is

225
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.100
that this original instances right now have 60%

226
00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.180
of the traffic

227
00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:21.300
and the replacement instances have 40% of the traffic.

228
00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:24.630
And so the traffic will be shifted gradually over time.

229
00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:27.630
And then after everything has been shifted

230
00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.810
they will check for stability for maybe five minutes.

231
00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:34.350
And then if everything is stable, the old task definition

232
00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.990
the test definition number 1 and the task set associated

233
00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:39.540
with a task definition will be deleted.

234
00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.420
And CodeDeploy will have successfully performed a deployment

235
00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:43.710
onto ECS.

236
00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.750
So you can see the ideas are very similar.

237
00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.210
It's just that every single service at its quirks.

238
00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:52.106
So CodeDeploy uses service features that are gonna be native

239
00:09:52.106 --> 00:09:54.840
to that service, but the idea remains the same.

240
00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:57.510
And so if you do understand these four kind of deployments

241
00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:00.180
you are all set for your essay Pro exam.

242
00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.170
All right, that's it.

243
00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:02.920
I will see you in the next lecture.

